WEBVTT

00:00.366 --> 00:02.366
Let’s start the Clawall demo.

00:03.566 --> 00:09.866
First, I run "openclaw clawall -h" to verify that Clawall is installed and accessible.

00:14.533 --> 00:22.333
Then I run "openclaw clawall wallet" to check wallet connectivity and confirm the runtime context is loaded.

00:29.600 --> 00:33.966
Wallet is loaded and shows all available coins with their balances.

00:34.533 --> 00:40.700
Now I run "npm run topup" to open the vault top-up flow.

00:41.400 --> 00:49.700
The script asks for the private key; mine is loaded from environment variables, and the vault ID is read from .env.

00:57.366 --> 01:01.966
Now you can select the desired coin and enter the amount to top up.

01:02.733 --> 01:06.266
Select 0 when the top-up is done to exit.

01:13.200 --> 01:19.466
Now I run "openclaw clawall transfer 0.0032 WAL to recipient_address"

01:27.133 --> 01:35.066
Transfer is now being processed on-chain; I’ll wait for confirmation and then verify the transaction details.

01:37.400 --> 01:39.900
Transfer executed successfully.

01:39.900 --> 01:45.033
I received the transaction digest and Explorer link for on-chain proof.

01:46.633 --> 01:49.000
let’s run it through the agent interface.

01:53.466 --> 01:57.400
we verify wallet balance and execute a test transfer.

02:17.000 --> 02:21.900
The agent shows the updated wallet balance, and the transaction is confirmed.

02:33.833 --> 02:38.966
We’ll also confirm the wallet balance in the terminal to cross-verify the result.

02:50.166 --> 02:54.066
Now we’ll run another transfer to validate the flow one more time.

03:01.400 --> 03:04.500
Request is submitted; we’ll wait for the agent’s response.

03:06.333 --> 03:11.666
Telegram alert was triggered due to frequent transfers to the same recipient in a short time.

03:12.433 --> 03:14.900
Now we will approve the transaction.

03:26.566 --> 03:31.433
Approval is received, and the transaction is now broadcast successfully.

03:38.700 --> 03:41.200
Now we’ll demonstrate a rejection scenario.

03:42.833 --> 03:49.000
We’ll now attempt a transfer, deny it in Telegram, and observe how Clawall blocks execution.

03:56.833 --> 04:03.400
We received a Telegram approval prompt for this high-risk transaction, and now we’re denying it.

04:07.266 --> 04:11.100
As expected, Clawall blocks the transfer through the governance layer.

04:17.700 --> 04:22.100
Now we’ll review the recent transaction history to verify the full decision trail.

04:23.700 --> 04:30.800
We got the 10 most recent transactions processed by Clawall, including digest and full execution details.

04:40.866 --> 04:45.000
We can also fetch and review logs directly through Telegram commands.

04:45.766 --> 04:49.600
We also received the full transaction details through Telegram.

04:56.100 --> 05:00.100
Now we’ll run the demo shell to simulate additional edge cases.

05:01.000 --> 05:06.733
We’ll now simulate a tamper attack, where Clawall detects that protected files were modified.

05:09.300 --> 05:13.500
Now we’ll attempt to execute a transaction and observe the policy decision.

05:19.633 --> 05:27.166
The transaction is blocked by policy enforcement, and we also receive a Telegram tamper alert confirming the integrity breach.

05:36.833 --> 05:40.066
Now we’ll verify the agent state using the demo shell.

05:41.000 --> 05:45.000
We’ll fix tamper mode first, then retry the transaction.

05:47.500 --> 05:52.266
Now that the hash is restored, the transaction should execute successfully.

05:54.666 --> 05:59.666
Since the agent is sending many transactions, we expect a Telegram approval prompt.

06:07.866 --> 06:12.900
Now we can see tamper mode is fixed, so the transaction executes successfully.

06:18.200 --> 06:20.833
Now we’ll check our wallet through the demo shell.

06:23.933 --> 06:26.300
We can see the wallet balance clearly.

06:28.866 --> 06:33.300
Now we’ll start the ClawAll local dashboard by running "npm run dashboard".

06:34.500 --> 06:41.033
Great, the dashboard server is up, and now we have the local dashboard link ready to open in the browser.

06:41.600 --> 06:46.133
Now I’ll open the dashboard in the browser so we can review the live operational view.

06:47.466 --> 06:50.633
We can see all transactions and live status here.

06:52.866 --> 06:56.700
Also here’s our vault balances and coin holdings, live.

06:56.700 --> 07:03.800
Here we can see the recent transactions, and each one includes an explorer link for quick verification.

07:05.966 --> 07:10.966
Perfect, the link opened and the transaction is confirmed on-chain.

07:13.000 --> 07:19.700
That’s the ClawAll dashboard: live risk, vault, and transaction visibility in one place.

07:20.266 --> 07:22.266
So that was the ClawAll demo.
